WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.900 --> 00:00:07.560
As you came, Bert, to the last two minutes and forty seconds, I think you had first down,

2
00:00:07.560 --> 00:00:11.280
ten to go on your own twenty-yard line and you’re facing an uphill battle.

3
00:00:11.280 --> 00:00:23.480
And I’d like to know the circumstances and the feeling and the communication among all of the men on the field at that time as you started that . . . what turned out to be a fateful drive.

4
00:00:23.480 --> 00:00:29.860
Well, I don’t think that we really were as aware of the circumstances as what we are now.

5
00:00:29.860 --> 00:00:35.500
There are not many occasions, certainly in my playing career, in high school, college and professional ranks,

6
00:00:35.500 --> 00:00:40.020
that you have the opportunity to do what we did on offense in that game.

7
00:00:40.020 --> 00:00:44.640
And that is that the game was yours if you could go and take it.

8
00:00:44.640 --> 00:00:49.340
And the whole thing was set on this one drive.

9
00:00:49.340 --> 00:00:56.860
And every play was just as important as what was going to be the next because we literally had to go eighty yards.

10
00:00:56.860 --> 00:00:59.100
We knew that we didn’t have much time.

11
00:00:59.100 --> 00:01:00.440
What did we have?

12
00:01:00.440 --> 00:01:02.200
One time-out left?

13
00:01:02.200 --> 00:01:03.540
Two [minutes] forty [seconds], yeah.

14
00:01:03.540 --> 00:01:07.020
And it was a lot of fun because we literally were playing hurry-up football.

15
00:01:07.020 --> 00:01:14.300
And we didn’t do that a lot, because we were a very strong football team and we didn’t have to do that.

16
00:01:14.300 --> 00:01:21.380
So it was an aggressive approach on offense, being able to throw the ball and, like you said, there were so many big plays.

17
00:01:21.380 --> 00:01:22.280
I think . . . What did we have?

18
00:01:22.280 --> 00:01:23.420
Two fourth-down plays?

19
00:01:23.420 --> 00:01:23.620
Three.

20
00:01:23.420 --> 00:01:23.620
Yeah.

21
00:01:23.420 --> 00:01:24.660
Three.

22
00:01:24.660 --> 00:01:25.260
See?

23
00:01:25.260 --> 00:01:33.500
Tyler’s memory’s a lot better than mine. [Ferguson laughs] But it was just a great feeling to go in and be able to have a drive,

24
00:01:33.500 --> 00:01:34.640
take the drive,

25
00:01:34.640 --> 00:01:41.180
go in and score and know that once you scored that you had won the ballgame of what possibly was going to be,

26
00:01:41.180 --> 00:01:45.820
and still can be, one of the biggest games of your whole entire life.
